{"text": " A lawyer in the US submitted AI-generated court cases to a judge. None of them existed. He nearly lost his license. The AI did not lie. It did exactly what it was designed to do. This lesson explains why and how to make sure it never happens to you. In this lesson Tolkien's hallucination and the verification habit that separates professionals from people who embarrass themselves with AI. We cover the Tolkien prediction system. Why AI generates one word at a time? What hallucination is and why it happens by design, not by accident? The verification habit that every professional AI user must have. And when to trust AI output completely and when to always verify. Every word you read from an AI was predicted one Tolkien at a time. A Tolkien is roughly one word or word fragment. The model looks at everything written before it and asks what is the most statistically likely word to come next. AI hallucination is not a bug. It is the model working exactly as designed. The model is optimized to produce fluent plausible text, not verified facts. Use AI for structure drafting, brainstorming and speed. Verify anything that will be relied upon professionally. Remember this. Never publish AI generated statistics, case citations, names or specific facts without independent verification. AI drafts you verify always. Apply what you learn in this lesson and you will see results immediately. Your skills bear fruit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.96, "text": " A lawyer in the US submitted AI-generated court cases to a judge.", "tokens": [50364, 316, 11613, 294, 264, 2546, 14405, 7318, 12, 21848, 770, 4753, 3331, 281, 257, 6995, 13, 50684], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17683622416327982, "compression_ratio": 1.4147727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 0.017651759088039398, "words": [{"word": " A", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.18, "probability": 0.8854296803474426}, {"word": " lawyer", "start": 0.18, "end": 0.52, "probability": 0.9954468607902527}, {"w